    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Water on a basement floor after a storm is usually assumed to have come in through the walls and run downhill. Often it did not. Water that appears in the middle of a slab, or that seeps steadily out of the seam where the floor meets the wall, came from underneath — pushed up by a water table that has risen above the bottom of your basement. That distinction changes the entire repair strategy.
        </p>

        <div id="hydrostatic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Hydrostatic Pressure, Briefly</h2>
          <p>
            Soil holds water, and water in soil behaves like water anywhere else: it exerts pressure proportional to its depth. When rain saturates the ground around a foundation faster than the soil can drain, the local water table rises. If it rises above the level of your basement floor, everything below that line is under pressure.
          </p>
          <p>
            The numbers are larger than people expect. A column of water eight feet deep exerts roughly 62 pounds per square foot at its base. Across a 1,000 square foot slab that is tens of thousands of pounds pushing upward, and a four-inch concrete slab poured on gravel is nowhere near a waterproof barrier. Water moves through pores, through shrinkage cracks, around plumbing penetrations, and above all through the cove joint — the seam between the slab edge and the footing, which is a cold joint by design and was never sealed.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is why blocking does not work. Seal one path and the pressure simply finds the next one. A coating applied to the underside of that pressure will blister and lift. The only durable strategy is to give the water somewhere better to go — which is exactly what a drain tile and sump system does, by lowering the water table below the slab before it can push.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">62 lb</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per sq ft at 8 ft of saturated soil</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">85%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Of wet basements start with surface drainage</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$5-15k</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Interior drain tile with sump</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">1,500 gal</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Roof runoff from one inch of rain</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="diagnose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Working Out Where It Is Coming From</h2>
          <p>
            Get on your hands and knees with a flashlight during or immediately after the next rain. Where water first appears tells you almost everything.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[560px]">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where Water Appears</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Most Likely Cause</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Fix</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Seam at floor and wall</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cove joint under pressure</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Interior drain tile and sump</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Middle of the slab</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High water table, slab cracks</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drain tile, crack injection</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">A specific crack</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shrinkage or settlement crack</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane injection</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Running down a wall first</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wall crack or window well</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Crack repair, well drainage</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Around a pipe penetration</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unsealed sleeve</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hydraulic cement or sealant</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Timing is diagnostic too. Water that appears within an hour of heavy rain is usually surface water reaching the foundation directly — gutters, downspouts, grade. Water that appears a day or two after a long wet spell is a rising water table, which is the classic hydrostatic case. Water that is present year round regardless of weather suggests a spring, a high permanent water table, or a plumbing leak.
          </p>
          <p>
            One thing worth ruling out early: condensation. A cool slab in a humid basement sweats, and the resulting dampness can look like seepage. Tape a square of plastic tightly to the floor and check it after 24 hours. Moisture on top of the plastic is condensation from the room air. Moisture underneath it is coming through the slab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cheap-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Check the Cheap Things First</h2>
          <p>
            A large share of wet basements are caused by roof water, not groundwater, and the fixes cost a few hundred dollars rather than several thousand. Before anyone quotes you an excavation, spend a weekend on these.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Downspouts</h3>
          <p>
            An inch of rain on a 1,500 square foot roof is roughly 900 gallons of water, and every downspout concentrates a share of that in one spot. Discharging two feet from the wall drops it directly into the soil column that presses against your foundation. Extend every downspout at least six feet, ideally ten, and check that the extensions are actually connected after winter.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gutters</h3>
          <p>
            A clogged gutter overflows at the wall, which is the worst possible place for it. Clean them twice a year and watch during an actual downpour rather than assuming.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Grade</h3>
          <p>
            Soil should fall roughly six inches over the first ten feet away from the house. Backfill settles over decades, and many houses now have a shallow trench around them that collects everything. Regrading with clay-rich soil is cheap and often decisive.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Window Wells</h3>
          <p>
            A well with a blocked drain becomes a bucket held against your basement wall. Clear the drain, add stone, and fit covers.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Do This Before Any Quote</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Any contractor who quotes an interior drain system without walking the exterior, looking at the downspouts and checking the grade is selling rather than diagnosing. Fix the surface water first, wait through one significant storm, and then reassess. Sometimes the problem is simply gone, and if it is not, you have removed a variable that would otherwise overload whatever system you install.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="solutions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Real Solutions and What They Cost</h2>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[600px]">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Solution</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Right For</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Downspouts and grading</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$200-2,000</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Storm-triggered flooding</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Crack injection</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$400-900 per crack</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One identifiable crack</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sump pump only</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,200-3,000</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Existing drain tile, failed pump</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interior drain tile and sump</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$5,000-15,000</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cove joint and slab seepage</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Exterior excavation system</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$15,000-30,000</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deteriorating foundation walls</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            For water coming up through the floor specifically, interior drain tile with a sump is the standard and correct answer. Exterior waterproofing is a superior system for protecting the <em>walls</em>, but it does far less for a slab under hydrostatic pressure, and it costs two to three times as much. Our guide to <a href="/interior-vs-exterior-basement-waterproofing/">interior versus exterior basement waterproofing</a> covers where each belongs, and the <a href="/basement-waterproofing-cost/">basement waterproofing cost guide</a> breaks down every method side by side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drain-tile" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How Interior Drain Tile Actually Works</h2>
          <p>
            The concept is simple and the execution is disruptive. A crew saw-cuts a channel roughly twelve inches wide around the interior perimeter of the basement, breaks out that concrete, and digs down beside the footing. Perforated pipe goes in on a bed of washed stone, wrapped so soil fines cannot clog it, and runs to a sump pit at the low point. The channel is backfilled with stone and the concrete is patched.
          </p>
          <p>
            The pipe sits below the slab, so as the water table rises it reaches the pipe before it reaches the underside of your floor. The pump lifts that water out and discharges it away from the house. The water table under the slab never gets high enough to push, and the cove joint stays dry because there is no pressure behind it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Good installations share several details worth checking in a quote. The pipe should be full perimeter or at minimum cover every wall that leaks. There should be a vapor barrier or wall membrane directing water from the wall into the channel rather than onto the floor. The sump pit should be a proper sealed basin with an airtight lid rather than an open hole. And the discharge must run well away from the foundation, never into a downspout line that will back up under the same storm.
          </p>
          <p>
            A battery backup pump is not optional in a home where the power fails during storms, which describes most homes. The storm that overwhelms your drainage is the storm that takes the power out, and a primary pump with no backup is a system that fails exactly when it is needed. Budget $400 to $1,200 for a backup unit and consider a water alarm as well.
          </p>
          <p>
            One realistic caveat: this system manages water rather than eliminating it. The basement stays dry, the pump runs during wet spells, and the pump is a mechanical device with a service life of roughly seven to ten years. Plan on replacing it, and test it a couple of times a year by pouring a bucket into the pit. Our guide to <a href="/interior-basement-waterproofing/">interior basement waterproofing</a> covers what a complete system includes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="doesnt-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Does Not Work</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Waterproof Paint on the Floor</h3>
          <p>
            Masonry waterproofing paint is rated for a few pounds of pressure. Hydrostatic pressure under a slab is many times that. The coating blisters, lifts in sheets, and the water reappears at the cove joint. Floor sealers and coatings have a legitimate role in reducing vapor and dampness on a slab that is otherwise managed, which is what our guide to <a href="/basement-floor-sealer/">basement floor sealers</a> covers — but they are a finishing step, not a water management strategy.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hydraulic Cement in the Cove Joint</h3>
          <p>
            Packing the floor-wall seam with hydraulic cement is a popular weekend fix and it fails predictably. The joint exists because the slab and the footing are separate pours that move independently. Rigid filler cracks, and in the meantime the water moves to the next weak point — often a slab crack in the middle of the room, which is a worse place to have it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Sump Pump With No Drain Tile</h3>
          <p>
            A pit dug in the corner with a pump in it collects only the water that happens to run to that corner. Without perimeter pipe to intercept water under the whole slab, the pump is a puddle remover rather than a solution.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Finishing the Basement and Hoping</h3>
          <p>
            Framing and drywall over a slab that takes water occasionally converts a manageable nuisance into mold inside a wall cavity you cannot see. Solve the water first. Every dollar spent on finish over an unresolved leak gets spent twice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="emergency" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What to Do Right Now, With Water on the Floor</h2>
          <p>
            Before anything else, deal with electricity. Do not walk into standing water in a basement with powered outlets, appliances or a furnace at floor level. If there is any doubt, shut the power off at the main from a dry position or call an electrician.
          </p>
          <p>
            Once it is safe, get the water out. A wet vacuum handles small volumes; a utility pump and a hose handle real flooding. Move stored belongings up off the floor and out of the room, prioritizing anything porous — cardboard, upholstery, paper — because those become mold substrates within a day or two.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then dry aggressively. Air movement matters more than heat: fans pointed across the slab and into corners, a dehumidifier running continuously, and doors open between spaces. The goal is to get everything dry within 48 hours, which is roughly the window before mold establishes. A basement dehumidifier is worth owning permanently in any house with this history — see our roundup of the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best basement dehumidifiers</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            While it is happening, document. Photograph where the water first appeared, how deep it got, and what the weather was doing. That record is what turns a vague conversation with a contractor into an accurate diagnosis, and it is what an insurer will ask for. Note the time relative to the rain, too — early means surface water, delayed means water table.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is water coming up through my basement floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hydrostatic pressure. When the soil under and around the foundation saturates, the water table rises and pushes water upward through the slab, through cracks, and most commonly through the cove joint where the floor meets the wall. The slab is not a waterproof membrane, so once pressure builds under it water finds every available path.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is water coming through the basement floor serious?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a serious moisture problem and rarely a structural emergency. Repeated saturation causes mold, ruins finished space and stored belongings, and can undermine the slab over time by washing out fines from the base. It is not usually a sign the foundation is failing, but it will not resolve on its own either.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you stop water from coming up through a basement floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You relieve the pressure rather than trying to block it. That means an interior drain tile system feeding a sump pump, which collects water below slab level and pumps it out before it can push upward. Sealing the slab alone does not work against hydrostatic pressure, because water simply finds the next weakest point.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to fix water coming up through a basement floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Exterior drainage corrections run $200 to $2,000 and solve a surprising share of cases. A sump pump installation is $1,200 to $3,000. A full interior perimeter drain tile system with a sump runs $5,000 to $15,000 depending on the perimeter length and whether the basement is finished.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal a basement floor to stop water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sealers help against vapor and light seepage, not against hydrostatic pressure. A densifier or epoxy coating reduces damp and stops the slab sweating, but water under real pressure will lift the coating or move to the cove joint. Seal the floor after the water is managed, not instead of managing it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my basement only flood in heavy rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because surface water is overwhelming the drainage around the house faster than the soil can move it away. Clogged gutters, downspouts discharging at the foundation, and grade that slopes toward the house all concentrate roof water beside the wall, where it saturates the soil and raises the local water table within hours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a sump pump stop water coming through the floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is connected to drain tile that reaches the water. A sump pump in a pit with no perimeter drain collects only what happens to reach that pit. Paired with an interior drain tile loop around the footing, it lowers the water table under the slab and stops the upward pressure at its source.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Expensive Mistakes in a Wet Basement</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating the Slab Instead of Draining It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint and epoxy cannot hold back hydrostatic pressure. The coating lifts, the water moves to the cove joint, and the money is gone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a System Before Fixing the Downspouts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Roof water is the cheapest variable to remove and the most likely culprit. Solve it first, then judge what is left.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Battery Backup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The storm that fills the pit is the storm that cuts the power. A pump with no backup is a system designed to fail on the worst night.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Finishing Over an Unsolved Leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Framing and drywall hide the water and grow mold inside the cavity. Finish only after a full wet season with a dry floor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

