        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Water coming through a basement floor</strong> is hydrostatic pressure, and pressure cannot be sealed away. The durable fix is interior drain tile feeding a sump pump with a battery backup, which lowers the water table under the slab so nothing pushes upward in the first place.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Before spending five figures, spend a weekend and a few hundred dollars on <strong class="text-white">downspout extensions, clean gutters and positive grade</strong>. Roof water is the single largest contributor to saturated soil beside a foundation, and removing it either solves the problem outright or cuts the load on whatever system you install next.
          </p>
